{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "observations": [
    {
      "key": "obs_1",
      "facet": "term",
      "statement": "histogram sort is an efficient 3-pass refinement of a bucket sort algorithm.",
      "start_line": 5,
      "end_line": 7
    },
    {
      "key": "obs_2",
      "facet": "mechanism",
      "statement": "The first pass counts the number of items for each bucket in an auxiliary array and makes a running total so each auxiliary entry is the number of preceding items.",
      "start_line": 7,
      "end_line": 10
    },
    {
      "key": "obs_3",
      "facet": "mechanism",
      "statement": "The second pass puts each item in its proper bucket according to the auxiliary entry for the key of that item.",
      "start_line": 10,
      "end_line": 11
    },
    {
      "key": "obs_4",
      "facet": "mechanism",
      "statement": "The last pass sorts each bucket.",
      "start_line": 11,
      "end_line": 12
    },
    {
      "key": "obs_5",
      "facet": "relation",
      "statement": "histogram sort is also known as interpolation sort.",
      "start_line": 12,
      "end_line": 13
    },
    {
      "key": "obs_6",
      "facet": "relation",
      "statement": "histogram sort is a generalization of bucket sort.",
      "start_line": 14,
      "end_line": 16
    },
    {
      "key": "obs_7",
      "facet": "relation",
      "statement": "histogram sort is a specialization of counting sort.",
      "start_line": 18,
      "end_line": 20
    },
    {
      "key": "obs_8",
      "facet": "other",
      "statement": "A bucket sort uses fixed-size buckets. A histogram sort sets up buckets of exactly the right size in a first pass. A counting sort is a histogram sort with one bucket per possible key value.",
      "start_line": 25,
      "end_line": 26
    },
    {
      "key": "obs_9",
      "facet": "property",
      "statement": "The run time is effectively O(n log log n).",
      "start_line": 30,
      "end_line": 32
    },
    {
      "key": "obs_10",
      "facet": "mechanism",
      "statement": "An approximate rank function R sorts data into n bins, where R is an integer valued function into [0, n-1], 0 ≤ R(x) ≤ n-1 for x in S, for some x,y in S, R(x)=0 and R(y)=n-1, and x < y implies R(x) ≤ R(y) for x,y in S.",
      "start_line": 32,
      "end_line": 36
    },
    {
      "key": "obs_11",
      "facet": "property",
      "statement": "Each bin then has, on average, 1 entry. Under some rather broad assumptions the number of entries in a bin will be Poisson distributed whence the observation that the sort is O(n log log n).",
      "start_line": 36,
      "end_line": 37
    },
    {
      "key": "obs_12",
      "facet": "mechanism",
      "statement": "Allocate an auxiliary integer array H indexed 0 … n-1. Make one pass through the data to count the number of items in each bin, recording the counts in H. The array H is then converted into a cumulative array so each entry in H specifies the beginning bin position of the bin contents in T. Make a second pass through the data. Copy each item x in S from S to T at H(R(x)), then increment H(R(x)) so the next item in the bin goes in the next location in T.",
      "start_line": 38,
      "end_line": 38
    },
    {
      "key": "obs_13",
      "facet": "mechanism",
      "statement": "For numeric data, there is a simple R function that works very well: Let min, max be the minimum and maximum of S. Then R(x) = n*(x - min)/(max-min).",
      "start_line": 39,
      "end_line": 39
    },
    {
      "key": "obs_14",
      "facet": "property",
      "statement": "This uses quite a bit of extra memory. For large data sets, there could be slow downs because of page faults. For large n it is more efficient to bound the number of bins.",
      "start_line": 40,
      "end_line": 40
    }
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "key": "claim_1",
      "status": "DERIVED_INTERPRETATION",
      "supported_by": [
        "obs_6",
        "obs_7",
        "obs_8"
      ],
      "statement": "histogram sort is positioned between bucket sort and counting sort: it generalizes bucket sort by using variable-sized buckets determined by a first pass, and it is a specialization of counting sort where counting sort uses one bucket per possible key value."
    },
    {
      "key": "claim_2",
      "status": "DERIVED_INTERPRETATION",
      "supported_by": [
        "obs_9",
        "obs_11",
        "obs_12"
      ],
      "statement": "The O(n log log n) runtime is derived from the assumption that bins contain on average 1 entry with a Poisson distribution of entries, resulting from the use of an approximate rank function R to distribute data into n bins."
    },
    {
      "key": "claim_3",
      "status": "DERIVED_INTERPRETATION",
      "supported_by": [
        "obs_14",
        "obs_12"
      ],
      "statement": "The algorithm's memory usage is high due to the auxiliary array H and the need to store items in a separate output array T, leading to potential page faults for large datasets."
    }
  ],
  "questions": []
}
